WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.235
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:03.870
We're here with
James Austin Johnson.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.439
I was listening to a podcast
that you were on.

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.774
And you were doing
your Trump impression,

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:12.378
talking about the sort of
late '90s, early 2000

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:13.979
New York alt-comedy scene.
-Yeah.

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:16.248
-And it was so hyperspecific,
and the first time we met,

00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:18.784
I asked you, like, how --
based on your age,

00:00:18.784 --> 00:00:21.187
I thought you must have been
so young when that was going on.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:22.488
How did you know about it?

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:24.490
-Well, I started doing stand-up
pretty early.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.861
I mean, the first time
I did my own stand-up,

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.462
I was like 14, maybe.
-Gotcha.

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:31.597
-Like, filling time
while they counted the votes

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:34.267
at a singing competition.
-Okay.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.803
-And so I started in comedy
really early.

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:38.705
I always wanted to do this.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:41.074
-How were you doing onstage
as a 14-year-old?

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:42.408
Were you doing okay with laughs?

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:47.113
-Weirdly, the first couple times
sometimes are amazing.

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:50.983
It's the next 90 times
that are train wrecks.

00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:53.486
Like, I remember I was, like,
performing at

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:55.121
the Christian college
that my dad works at,

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.890
and my first time ever
doing my own comedy,

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.561
and I did this thing
where I was the Chuck E. Ch--

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.062
you know, the Chuck E. Cheese
robots?

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:05.264
-Mm-hmm.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:06.733
-I did this thing where I was,
like --

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.568
I was afraid to go to
Chuck E. Cheese as a kid

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:09.868
'cause I was afraid of
the characters.

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:10.970
They scared me.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.739
So when the robots
would perform,

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:15.274
they say, "It's your birthday."

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:16.809
I heard a different song.

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:19.545
Then I'd play "Another Brick in
the Wall Part 2" by Pink Floyd.

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:23.516
[ Laughter ]
Then I'd do the --

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:26.051
And that's what I was doing
as a 14-year-old.

00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:27.386
-That's very advanced
for a 14-year-old.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:30.289
-Pretty advanced. Really weird.

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:33.593
-You were recognized for
your comedy very early.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:36.562
You won a superlative
in high school.

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:38.598
You were the wittiest person.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:41.834
I will say, when I first glanced
at this, I did read "whitest."

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:44.303
-Yeah, I could have qualified.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:46.072
-You could have won them both.
-I could have qualified.

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:47.807
You know, they really had to
mess with the aperture

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:51.577
on the camera to pick up
some of my skin tones.

00:01:51.577 --> 00:01:52.645
-But this is good.

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:54.413
This means that
even as a young man,

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:57.984
your classmates, your peers
knew you to be comedy.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.552
-Yes.

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:03.890
It was pretty clear what my
ambitions were to everyone else.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.692
That was -- When the yearbook
committee came up to me

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:09.295
and they said,
"You got wittiest,"

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.064
I was like, "It does not get
better than this."

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:12.232
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:15.134
"I am going to be the funniest
poetry professor

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:16.970
Middle Tennessee has ever seen."

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.471
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.340
-What about your early
impressions as a kid?

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.275
Who were you doing then?
-Oh, my gosh.

00:02:23.275 --> 00:02:30.850
I mean, Jar Jar Binks remains an
important inspiration to us all.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.652
-Yeah.
-Especially me.

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:35.021
So I was doing a lot of
Jar Jar Binks.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.589
My parents always had big groups
of friends over all the time.

00:02:37.589 --> 00:02:39.592
They love entertaining,
and so they would drag me

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:41.593
out of my room and make me do
impressions for everybody.

00:02:41.593 --> 00:02:45.932
And the big three were
Jar Jar Binks, Al Gore,

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:47.967
Louis Armstrong.
-Gotcha.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.769
-And I took the trumpet out
for Louis Armstrong.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:50.869
-You did?
You had your own trumpet?

00:02:50.869 --> 00:02:52.771
-I would play a very bad bar of

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:55.074
"When the Saints
Go Marching In."

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.610
-And then did you start
doing open mikes?

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:01.147
-Yeah. So I went to
this Christian college,

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:03.749
where I had to sign a paper
saying that I won't sin ever.

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:06.485
-Okay.
-And so I was like, okay, good.

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:07.987
And that means you couldn't go
to bars

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:09.389
at the school that I went to.

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:12.959
And when I decided
that I needed to go full force

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:16.162
into stand-up professionally,
I Googled, like,

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:18.564
"stand-up open mike near me."

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:21.400
And it was always the most
just scum-of-the-Earth,

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.703
rat-den dive bars.

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:25.772
And I'm 19.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.175
I go to the Christian college
down the road.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:32.945
And I'm literally walking in
through patio doors

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:37.382
and getting a $5 Pepsi,
hoping not to get carded,

00:03:37.382 --> 00:03:40.286
and then telling my jokes that
no one wanted to hear.

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.321
-Yeah.
-And I did that for

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:45.925
like three years before -- like
two years before I turned 21

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.227
and could legally be in there.

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:51.164
-Did the paper you signed ever
come back to haunt you?

00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:52.632
Did they ever shake it
in your face?

00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:55.968
-I think someone is hammering on
my dad's door right now,

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:59.372
now that I've made this
admission on national TV.

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:02.140
But you, sir, who is in charge
of the ethics

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.743
at the Christian college,
what are you doing up so late?

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:08.781
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:10.416
-There you go.
-Think about it.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:12.985
Think about your choices.
-Think about your choices.

00:04:12.985 --> 00:04:15.488
Well, I'm glad you made
the choices you did, James.

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.323
It's absolutely wonderful
having you on "SNL"

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.457
and it's wonderful having you
here tonight.

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:20.459
You guys, that's
James Austin Johnson.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.896
"SNL," Saturday at 11:30 on NBC
and Peacock

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:26.566
with Willem Dafoe
and musical guest Katy Perry.

